# Latent Storage Benchmark (`lsbench`)

**How far can VAE latents be compressed before the decoded images or videos degrade?**

Modern generative pipelines store VAE latents (for training caches, datasets, archives), usually as `fp32`/`bf16` tensors. A 1024x1024 FLUX.2 latent is 2 MiB in `fp32`, larger than the PNG it came from. This benchmark measures **reconstruction quality vs. storage size and I/O time** for many latent storage schemes, from plain casts to block-wise quantization with learned codebooks, across several image and video VAEs.

It can also be used as a plain VAE benchmark (`fp32` storage = no compression).

## Key findings

- **Storage format is a free lunch down to ~5 bits per value.** On FLUX.2, 8-bit and `fp16`/`bf16` storage are indistinguishable from `fp32` on every metric, and 5.5 bits per value is still within noise (FID 2.15 vs 2.08, PSNR -0.07 dB).
- **The best scheme found is block-wise min/max quantization with a Lloyd-Max codebook** (`lloyd_minmax_square`). At 4.5 bits per value it is **7.1x smaller than `fp32`** (3.5x smaller than `fp16`) for -0.25 dB PSNR and +0.3 FID.
- **Ranking of schemes is almost VAE-independent** (Spearman rank correlation >= 0.97 on PSNR across five image VAEs, >= 0.98 across three video VAEs). Absolute sensitivity differs: e.g. 4-bit Lloyd-Max costs 0.15-0.2 dB PSNR on SD 3.5 and FLUX.2 but about 1 dB on SDXL and Qwen-Image 2.1.
- **What makes it work** (ablation on FLUX.2): block-wise min/max ranges give most of the gain over global min/max, and Lloyd-Max level tables help mainly at 2-3 bits.

![PSNR vs bits per value](results/lsbench_1.2/plots/ablation/flux_2_psnr_vs_bits.png)
![FID vs bits per value](results/lsbench_1.2/plots/ablation/flux_2_fid_vs_bits.png)

*FLUX.2 VAE, 1000 images at 1024x1024. Lines are Pareto fronts of the three feature sets (global min/max only, + block-wise min/max, + Lloyd-Max tables); black squares are naive formats. Bits per value is `bytes * 8 / (32 * 128 * 128)` and includes all metadata.*

| Storage (FLUX.2) | Bits / value | KiB / image | vs `fp32` | PSNR (dB) | SSIM | LPIPS | FID |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `fp32` (reference) | 32.0 | 2048 | 1.0x | 31.749 | 0.8711 | 0.0260 | 2.08 |
| `fp16` / `bf16` | 16.0 | 1024 | 2.0x | 31.749 / 31.748 | 0.8711 | 0.0260 | 2.08 |
| `fp8_e4m3` | 8.0 | 512 | 4.0x | 31.672 | 0.8706 | 0.0263 | 2.14 |
| `int8_tensor` | 8.0 | 512 | 4.0x | 31.733 | 0.8710 | 0.0260 | 2.10 |
| Lloyd-Max, 6 bit, block 4 | 6.5 | 417 | 4.9x | 31.732 | 0.8710 | 0.0260 | 2.10 |
| Lloyd-Max, 5 bit, block 4 | 5.5 | 353 | 5.8x | 31.684 | 0.8705 | 0.0263 | 2.15 |
| Lloyd-Max, 4 bit, block 4 | 4.5 | 289 | 7.1x | 31.501 | 0.8687 | 0.0272 | 2.39 |
| Lloyd-Max, 3 bit, block 4 | 3.5 | 225 | 9.1x | 30.836 | 0.8609 | 0.0312 | 3.31 |
| Lloyd-Max, 2 bit, block 4 | 2.5 | 161 | 12.8x | 28.753 | 0.8313 | 0.0496 | 7.84 |

*Lloyd-Max rows are `lloyd_minmax_square` with `block: 4, range_bits: 4`. FID is computed between the originals and the reconstructions, so even `fp32` has a non-zero floor caused by the VAE itself; compare against the `fp32` row.*

Serialization of the Lloyd-Max scheme costs roughly 1.5-3.5 ms per 1024x1024 image on a GPU (about 0.3 ms for uniform quantizers) and deserialization about 0.2 ms.

Full tables and plots for every phase are in [results/](results/).

## What is benchmarked

Pipeline for every VAE:

```
image/video -> vae.encode -> storage.serialize -> bytes -> storage.deserialize -> vae.decode -> metrics(original, reconstruction)
```

The size of `bytes` is the reported storage size, so every storage must put everything needed for decoding (shapes, scales, codebooks) into the byte string. All `no_vae` rows apply the same storage schemes directly to raw pixels and serve as a sanity baseline.

### VAEs (5 image + 3 video, plus `no_vae` identity baselines)

| Name | Domain | Latent channels, compression | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| `flux_2` | image | 32 ch, 8x spatial | [FLUX.2-klein-4B](https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.2-klein-4B) |
| `qwen_image_2_1` | image | 64 ch, 16x spatial | [texture-fix-vae-for-qwen-image-2.1](https://huggingface.co/madebyollin/texture-fix-vae-for-qwen-image-2.1) |
| `flux_1` | image | 16 ch, 8x spatial | [FLUX.1-schnell](https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-schnell) |
| `sd_3_5` | image | 16 ch, 8x spatial | [Stable Diffusion 3.5 Large](https://huggingface.co/stabilityai/stable-diffusion-3.5-large) |
| `sdxl` | image | 4 ch, 8x spatial | [sdxl-vae](https://huggingface.co/stabilityai/sdxl-vae) |
| `wan` | video | 16 ch, 8x spatial, 4x temporal | [Wan2.1](https://huggingface.co/Wan-AI/Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers) |
| `ltx_2_5` | video | 128 ch, 32x spatial, 8x temporal | [LTX-2.5](https://huggingface.co/Lightricks/LTX-2.5-Diffusers) |
| `minimax_h3` | video | 24 ch, 16x spatial, ~3.4x temporal | [MiniMax-H3](https://huggingface.co/MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3) |

All wrappers return **normalized latents** (the VAE's own scale/shift/BN statistics are applied inside the wrapper), which is what a storage would see in a real training pipeline.

### Latent storages (34 image + 14 video implementations, 200+ configurations)

| Family | Config `name`s | Idea |
|---|---|---|
| Floating point | `fp32`, `fp16`, `bf16`, `fp8_e4m3`, `fp8_e5m2`, `fp8_e4m3_scaled`, `mxfp8` | casts; per-sample or per-32-block (OCP MX) scaling |
| INT8 | `int8_tensor`, `int8_channel`, `int8_group`, `int8_asym`, `int8_mse_clip`, `int8_outlier`, `int8_hadamard`, `int8_mulaw`, `int8_minmax_square`, `nf8` | symmetric/asymmetric scales at different granularity, clipping, outlier side-channel, Hadamard rotation, mu-law companding |
| INT4 | `int4_group`, `int4_asym_group`, `int4_asym_square`, `int4_minmax_group`, `int4_minmax_square`, `int4_mse_clip`, `int4_hadamard`, `int4_outlier` | the same ideas at 4 bits (GPTQ/AWQ, QuaRot, SqueezeLLM style) |
| 4-bit float / codebook | `nvfp4`, `mxfp4`, `nf4`, `nf4_dq`, `kmeans4` | FP4 E2M1 with block scales, NormalFloat (QLoRA), k-means codebook |
| Other bit widths | `int2_minmax_square`, `int6_minmax_square` | block-wise min/max at 2 and 6 bits |
| Block-wise min/max + codebook | `lloyd_minmax_square`, `normal_cdf_minmax_square`, video: `lloyd_minmax_cube` | see below |

Naming: *group* = consecutive values in memory order, *square* = `block x block` spatial patch of one channel, *cube* = `block³` spatio-temporal patch (video).

**`lloyd_minmax_square`** (the best scheme found) splits every channel into `block x block` patches. For each patch it stores the min and max as `range_bits`-bit indices into the channel's global [min, max] range, and quantizes the values inside the patch to `bits`-bit codes. The `2^bits` reconstruction levels are a per-sample Lloyd-Max (1D k-means) table stored in the bytes (or uniform levels with `lloyd: false`; no blocks with `block: null`). Result names encode the hyperparameters, e.g. `lloyd_minmax_square_c4_b4_r4` = 4 bit codes, 4x4 blocks, 4 bit ranges; `minmax_square_*` is the same without Lloyd tables.

### Metrics

| | Image | Video |
|---|---|---|
| Pixel | MSE, PSNR | MSE, PSNR (over the whole video) |
| Structural | SSIM | SSIM (per frame) |
| Perceptual | LPIPS (AlexNet) | LPIPS (per frame) |
| Distribution | FID (Inception, 2048-d) | FVD (I3D, StyleGAN-V weights) |
| Efficiency | bytes per image, serialize / deserialize ms per image | bytes per video, serialize / deserialize ms per video |

PSNR, SSIM and LPIPS are averaged per sample. FID/FVD compare the set of originals with the set of reconstructions.

### Datasets

| Name | Domain | Used samples | Preparation |
|---|---|---|---|
| [axis-v1_1k](https://huggingface.co/datasets/refine-axis/axis-v1_1k) | image | 1000 images in 8 categories (animals, architecture, cinematic, humans, nature, night/neon, robotics, vehicles) | center crop + resize to 1024x1024 |
| [movirec](https://huggingface.co/datasets/madebyollin/movirec) | video | 500 clips | first 75 frames, center crop + resize to 256x256 |

## Benchmark phases

Running every storage on every VAE is expensive (and unnecessary), so the benchmark is staged, each stage narrowing the question of the previous one. Each stage has its own config in [configs/](configs/) and result folder in [results/](results/).

| Phase | Config | Question | VAEs | Storage configs | Metrics |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Broad search | [lsbench_1.0.yaml](configs/lsbench_1.0.yaml) | Which families of algorithms are worth keeping? | FLUX.2 (+ `no_vae`) | 73 (all families) | MSE, FID |
| 2. Generalization | [lsbench_1.1.yaml](configs/lsbench_1.1.yaml) | Do the best algorithms rank the same on other VAEs and on video? | 5 image + 3 video (+ `no_vae`) | 20 image / 24 video | MSE, PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, FID / FVD |
| 3. Ablation | [lsbench_1.2.yaml](configs/lsbench_1.2.yaml) | Which features of the best algorithm matter? | FLUX.2 | 96 | MSE, PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, FID |

Not every (VAE, storage, metric) combination is computed on purpose:

1. **Phase 1** compares many algorithms from different families (floating point, per-tensor/channel/group integer, Hadamard rotation, outlier side-channels, NormalFloat/FP4, codebooks, block-wise min/max with Lloyd-Max tables) on a *single* image VAE, only to filter out the worst performers. Early findings: all INT8 variants are equivalent; at 4 bits, rotation (Hadamard) hurts, per-channel Lloyd tables give nothing over per-sample tables but are 10x slower to fit, and block-wise min/max + Lloyd-Max tables dominates the quality/size front.
2. **Phase 2** applies a hand-picked subset of the best algorithm of each family to different VAEs, image and video. The goal is to show that performance of storage algorithms is highly correlated and mostly VAE-independent, so conclusions of phase 1/3 transfer. Spearman rank correlation of the storage ranking between VAE pairs:

   | Metric | Min over VAE pairs | Notes |
   |---|---:|---|
   | image PSNR | 0.974 | 5 VAEs |
   | image FID | 0.928 | 5 VAEs |
   | video PSNR | 0.986 | 3 VAEs |
   | video LPIPS | 0.982 | 3 VAEs |
   | video FVD | 0.630 | noisy: 500 clips, LTX-2.5 deviates most |

   What does differ is *sensitivity*, e.g. 4-bit Lloyd-Max (`c4_b4_r5`) costs between 0.15 dB (SD 3.5, FLUX.2) and 1.1 dB (SDXL, Qwen-Image 2.1) of PSNR.
3. **Phase 3** takes the final best algorithm and benchmarks it with many settings (bits 2-8, block 2/4/8/global, range bits 4/6, Lloyd tables on/off) to isolate which feature produces which gain. Again for a single VAE, as phase 2 showed that the result transfers.

Phase 1 predates the PSNR/SSIM/LPIPS metrics and only has MSE and FID.

## Setup

Requires Linux, Python >= 3.12, and a CUDA GPU (CPU works but is very slow for large VAEs).

```bash
uv venv --python=3.12
source .venv/bin/activate
# adjust the index URL to your CUDA version
uv pip install --pre torch torchvision torchaudio --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu134
uv pip install -r requirements.txt
uv pip install -e .
```

### Datasets

```bash
cd datasets
git clone https://huggingface.co/datasets/refine-axis/axis-v1_1k   # images
git clone https://huggingface.co/datasets/madebyollin/movirec      # videos
cd ..

# one-time: crops/resizes into prepared_datasets/, the only folder the loaders read
python scripts/prepare_datasets.py configs/lsbench_1.1.yaml
```

### Models

Only the `vae/` subfolders are needed. Some repositories are gated: accept the license on the model page and run `hf auth login` first (SD 3.5, LTX-2.5, MiniMax-H3).

```bash
cd models

# image VAEs
git clone https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.2-klein-4B
git clone https://huggingface.co/madebyollin/texture-fix-vae-for-qwen-image-2.1
git clone https://huggingface.co/stabilityai/sdxl-vae
hf download black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-schnell --include "vae/*" --local-dir FLUX.1-schnell
hf download stabilityai/stable-diffusion-3.5-large --include "vae/*" --local-dir stable-diffusion-3.5-large

# video VAEs
hf download Wan-AI/Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers --include "vae/*" --local-dir Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers
hf download Lightricks/LTX-2.5-Diffusers --include "vae/*" --local-dir LTX-2.5-Diffusers
hf download MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3 --include "vae/*" --local-dir MiniMax-H3

# I3D detector for the FVD metric
mkdir -p i3d && curl -L -o i3d/i3d_torchscript.pt "https://www.dropbox.com/s/ge9e5ujwgetktms/i3d_torchscript.pt?dl=1"
```

## Usage

```bash
# run a benchmark (resumable: finished rows are skipped, new config entries are computed)
python scripts/run_benchmark.py configs/lsbench_1.2.yaml

# quick smoke test on a few samples (results/lsbench_fast/, not tracked by git)
python scripts/run_benchmark.py configs/lsbench_fast.yaml --overwrite

# re-make plots from a results.parquet
python scripts/make_report.py results/lsbench_1.1

# ablation plots of phase 3
python scripts/make_ablation_plots.py results/lsbench_1.2
```

Each run writes to `results/<benchmark_name>/`:

- `results.parquet`: long-format table (`domain, vae, storage, metric, value`), the source of truth
- `tables/<domain>_<metric>.md`: VAE x storage pivots
- `plots/<vae>/` and `plots/all_vaes/`: metric vs size plots with Pareto fronts, plus a weighted summary score
- `config.yaml`: the config used

Results are keyed by (VAE, storage, metric) only. After changing datasets, `num_samples` or other data settings, use `--overwrite` or a new `benchmark_name`, otherwise old and new rows get mixed.

## Adding your own VAE, storage or metric

Components are registered by file + config entry only (no changes to the runner). See [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) for the exact interface contracts; it is written to be given to an AI coding assistant as well.

1. Add `src/lsbench/<image|video>/<vaes|latent_storages|metrics>/<name>_<kind>.py` with a class constant `NAME`.
2. Reference `NAME` (optionally with constructor arguments) in a config's `vaes` / `latent_storages` / `metrics` list.
3. Smoke test with a copy of `configs/lsbench_fast.yaml`.

## Notes and limitations

- Single run per configuration, no confidence intervals; timings are from one GPU and indicative only.
- The FLUX.2 wrapper encodes with `latent_dist.sample()` (stochastic) while all other VAEs use `.mode()`. All storages of one run see the same latents, so comparisons are fair, but absolute FLUX.2 numbers vary slightly between runs.
- Only reconstruction quality is measured, not the effect of quantized latents on downstream training or generation.
- Quality metrics are measured against the original image, so VAE error is included in all numbers.

